By~\eqref{eq:pu}--\eqref{eq:pb0} and~\eqref{eq:pbarvals}, the three marginals and the surprisal under $\bar{P}$ are constant on the categories~\eqref{eq:cats}, the values of the latter being reported in Table~\ref{tab:catscores}.
Hence, for $B_{\circ} \in \{ B_U, B_0, B_1 \}$ and $m = 1, 2$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:byterule}
\begin{aligned}
E \left[ \lambda \left( \bar{P}(B_{\circ}) \right)^{m} \right] &= \sum_{v \in \mathcal{V}} P_{B_{\circ}}(v) \lambda \left( \bar{P}(v) \right)^{m} \\
&= \sum_{\gamma \in \Gamma} \lvert \gamma \rvert P_{B_{\circ}}(\gamma) \lambda \left( \bar{P}(\gamma) \right)^{m}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The category partitioning thus reduces each expectation to four summands since all $\lvert \gamma \rvert$ bytes of a given category contribute the same term.

For the exponent pair the weights must instead come from the joint law $P_{B_{0,1}}$.
It is uniform on $\mathcal{X}$ by~\eqref{eq:pb01}, and the surprisal depends on $(v_0, v_1)$ only through their categories, so, to compute a pair moment, only the number of admissible pairs in each cell of $\Gamma \times \Gamma$ is needed:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cells}
n_{\gamma_0 \gamma_1} =
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
\gamma_0 \big\backslash \gamma_1 & \mathcal{V}^{*}_{lo} & \{127\} & \mathcal{V}^{*}_{hi} & \{255\} \\
\hline
\mathcal{V}^{*}_{lo} & 16129 & 127 & 16129 & 127 \\
\{127\} & 127 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
\mathcal{V}^{*}_{hi} & 16129 & 127 & 16129 & 127 \\
\{255\} & 127 & 1 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\end{equation}
Every cell holds all $\lvert \gamma_0 \rvert \lvert \gamma_1 \rvert$ pairs of $\gamma_0 \times \gamma_1$, except the four that the IEEE-754 validity constraint discards.

A moment of the exponent pair therefore reduces to a sixteen-term sum over $\Gamma \times \Gamma$. For $m = 1,2$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pairrule}
\begin{aligned}
E \left[ \left( \lambda \left( \bar{P}(B_0) \right) + \lambda \left( \bar{P}(B_1) \right) \right)^{m} \right]  &= \!\!\! \sum_{(v_0, v_1) \in \mathcal{X}} \!\!\! P_{B_{0,1}}(v_0, v_1) \left( \lambda \left( \bar{P}(v_0) \right) + \lambda \left( \bar{P}(v_1) \right) \right)^{m} \\
&= \frac{1}{65280} \sum_{\gamma_0, \gamma_1 \in \Gamma} n_{\gamma_0 \gamma_1} \left( \lambda \left( \bar{P}(\gamma_0) \right) + \lambda \left( \bar{P}(\gamma_1) \right) \right)^{m},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The common denominator factors out, while $n_{\gamma_0 \gamma_1}$ gives the multiplicity of each category pair.

